{"profile":{"name":"Olivia Chow","ward":"Mayor of Toronto (city-wide); formerly Councillor for Ward 20, Trinity-Spadina (1998-2005)","bio":"Olivia Chow was elected the 66th Mayor of Toronto in the June 26, 2023 mayoral by-election and assumed office on July 12, 2023, becoming the first person of Asian descent and the first woman since amalgamation to hold the office. Her political career includes serving as a Toronto school board trustee (1985-1991), a Metro/Toronto city councillor for Trinity-Spadina (1992-2005), and as the NDP Member of Parliament for Trinity-Spadina (2006-2014). She immigrated to Toronto from Hong Kong at age 13 and is the widow of former NDP leader Jack Layton.","committees":["Executive Committee (Chair)"],"contact":{"email":"mayor_chow@toronto.ca","phone":"416-397-2489"},"promises":[{"text":"Build 25,000 rent-controlled homes over the next eight years on city-owned land, including at least 7,500 affordable units and at least 2,500 rent-geared-to-income units, with the city acting as developer.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-mayoral-election-top-candidates-on-housing/"},{"text":"Raise the Vacant Home Tax to 3% and use the funds collected to construct affordable and subsidized units and support the City's existing affordable housing initiatives, including rent supplements.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-mayoral-election-top-candidates-on-housing/"},{"text":"Create a Toronto Renters Action Committee, a task force dedicated to establishing anti-renoviction bylaws, advocating for real rent control, and reviewing existing policies and programs related to renters.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-mayoral-election-top-candidates-on-housing/"}]},"lobbying":{"total":284,"term":284,"first":"2023-07-12","last":"2026-10-08","lobbyists":103,"clients":56,"subjects":156,"methods":{"Meetings":88,"Emails":163,"Phone calls":13,"Written":16,"Other or unreported":4},"top":[{"label":"Economic Development","count":67},{"label":"By-law / Regulati